From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 21:44:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: exynos: Add initial support for the Exynos9820
 SoC

Add a minimal device tree for the Exynos9820: the eight CPUs, PSCI, the
GIC, the system timer, CMU_TOP, CMU_APM, CMU_PERIS and CMU_PERIC0, the
eight pin controllers, the debug UART and the HSI2C buses the board
needs. Everything else the SoC has is left out rather than described and
disabled, because a node nobody drives is a claim nobody checks.

Both files are transcriptions of the vendor sources. For the pin
controller that means the bank names and their order, which the driver
has to agree with, and the wakeup interrupt numbers the SoC wires to the
GIC. For the SoC file it means every address, interrupt number and clock
index, with the nodes under soc@0 ordered by unit address. Nothing here
is carried over from another port without that check.

The SoC gets its own MAINTAINERS section. The clock driver, its binding
and the device trees would otherwise fall through to the subsystem wide
entries, and get_maintainer.pl would name nobody who knows this part.
The pin controller tables are not listed there: they live in files that
already have an owner.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
